        "statement": "Assume the standing full AC convention. Give compact oriented smooth manifolds finite cellular models, and give their cellular complexes the local regular coefficients of any discrete group $G$. Put $A=\\mathcal N G$. For each degree, let $p_Z$ and $p_B$ be the Hilbert projections onto cycles and the closure of boundaries after completion in $\\ell^2(G)$. The algebraic reduced part is the finite projective $A$-module $P=(p_Z-p_B)A^n$, whereas completed reduced homology is the Hilbert module $H=(p_Z-p_B)\\ell^2(G)^n$. Algebraic homology with $A$ coefficients surjects onto $P$ with zero-dimensional kernel; its natural map into $H$ has dense image $P$ under $x\\mapsto x\\delta_e$, and need not be surjective onto $H$. The Hilbert completion of $P$ in this embedding is $H$. Pair and Mayer–Vietoris sequences become weakly exact on completed reduced homology: the closure of each image is the next kernel. Poincaré–Lefschetz cap duality induces bounded invertible maps on completed reduced homology and cohomology. In particular, the middle intersection operator of a closed oriented even-dimensional manifold is invertible. The cap boundary identity and the intersection/cup identities persist on these completed modules, with their ordinary orientation signs.",
